          "kids": "Homo floresiensis was a tiny human relative from the island of Flores in Indonesia. Adults were only about 1 metre tall, as short as a young child today! They lived there for hundreds of thousands of years, along with dwarf elephant relatives, giant rats and Komodo dragons.",
          "clinician": "Small-bodied, small-brained hominin from Liang Bua (skeletal remains ~100–60 ka; artefacts ~190–50 ka; Sutikna et al. 2016) and Mata Menge (~700 ka; van den Bergh et al. 2016; Brumm et al. 2016), where a small adult humerus shows that small body size had evolved by then (Kaifu et al. 2024). Proposed origins: insular dwarfing of an early H. erectus population, or descent from an earlier small-bodied hominin dispersal (Argue et al. 2017). Pathological explanations of LB1 are rejected by most researchers. Brain size and stature are LB1 estimates (426 cm³: Kubo et al. 2013; ~106 cm: Brown et al. 2004)."
